mething else. DRAFT it (do not create it; the machinery mints your draft at this row's own close) as a fenced `toml` block under a `## Dispositions` heading in THIS SESSION'S OWN SPEC — never in the verdict file — written as TOP-LEVEL `key = value` lines (no `[table]` header; intake refuses an unknown table key), carrying at minimum `title` (at most 120 characters — a concise name, rationale goes in the prose beside the block), `workstream`, `buildtier`, and `supersedes = \"<the closed WI id>\"` so the partial work keeps its thread across the id change. The mint REPLACES the superseded row's inbound hard `needs` edges with the successor, so nothing is left waiting on the terminal row.\n \n-4. **What does the successor cost?** Estimate `buildtier` (`quick` | `medium` | `strong`) from what is left to do — the remaining scope, not the frustration in the report — and set `planmode = \"dual\"` only when the remaining work is a genuine design fork rather than a build.\n+4. **Is the answer human-owed?** Where a genuine part of what is left is the OWNER's to rule and you found no alternative route, add `open_item = \"<the one-line question>\"` to the successor's block. The machinery mints a `pending` open item (id from the watermark's OI space) and lands its id in the successor's `needs`, so the successor cannot become ready until the owner rules — the ruling gates the work, not your restraint. This is a TYPED DEPENDENCY of the successor, never a standalone exit. Then estimate `buildtier` (`quick` | `medium` | `strong`) from what is left to do — the remaining scope, not the frustration in the report — and set `planmode = \"dual\"` only when the remaining work is a genuine design fork rather than a build.\n \n Write your verdict to {verdict} in the log.md block format: one `- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <what> -> why -> the concrete change -> @owner` line per finding. Then exactly one machine line:\n \n     OUTCOME: COMPLETE|PARTIAL|CANCELLED successors=N\n \n-`OUTCOME` is YOUR ruling on what the close means, which may differ from the lane's claim; `successors` is the number of `## Dispositions` blocks you drafted. Commit that verdict file (an adjudication is a recorded verdict — its one home), ending that commit with the trailer `WI: {wi}` — the coordinator learns a judgement is recorded from that trailer and from nothing else, so a verdict committed without it leaves this row open. THEN CLOSE YOUR OWN ROW (C6 — an adjudication lane whose spec stays in active/ is resumed forever, each resume a fresh judge finding nothing to judge): fill this spec's `## Deliverable` body (placed BEFORE `## Context` — after it parses as empty) with the `OUTCOME:` line and the verdict path; clear the spec's `specref` line; move the spec with `python scripts/spec_move.py` to docs/archive/work/complete/; and end that close commit with the same `WI: {wi}` trailer. The CLOSED ROW YOU JUDGED is untouchable — never move it, never edit its scope; the only spec you close is this adjudication row's own.\n+`OUTCOME` is YOUR ruling on what the close means, which may differ from the lane's claim; `successors` is the number of `## Dispositions` blocks you drafted (at least one for a PARTIAL or CANCELLED ruling). Commit that verdict file (an adjudication is a recorded verdict — its one home), ending that commit with the trailer `WI: {wi}` — the coordinator learns a judgement is recorded from that trailer and from nothing else, so a verdict committed without it leaves this row open. YOU DO NOT MOVE YOUR OWN SPEC: once your verdict is recorded, the machinery CLOSES this row for you (it fills a `## Deliverable`, clears `specref`, archives the spec into `complete/`, and mints your `## Dispositions` drafts into `queued/` at the merge). A disposition that recorded a verdict but drafted NO successor is REFUSED at that close and the run stops for a human — so draft the successor before you commit. The CLOSED ROW YOU JUDGED is untouchable — never move it, never edit its scope; and the only successors that exist are the ones you drafted in THIS row's `## Dispositions` section.\ndiff --git a/project-trajectory/registries/work-items.template.csv b/project-trajectory/registries/work-items.template.csv\nindex 2f925879..cdf5527f 100644\n--- a/project-trajectory/registries/work-items.template.csv\n+++ b/project-trajectory/registries/work-items.template.csv\n@@ -1,2 +1,2 @@\n WI-ID,Title,Workstream,SR-Refs,Predecessors,Status,Deliverable,SpecRef,BuildTier,CritiqueBudget,CritiqueExhaustion,Priority,Exclusive,BlockRef,EstTokens,SafetyClass,PlanMode,Bar,Supersedes,Brief\n-WI-000,EXAMPLE - delete on first real entry,workstream-name,SR-000,,queued,\"How the work executes (the artifact this ships). Workstream = the mutable grouping category the work advances (the legacy 'Track' header is still read); SR-Refs = the requirement id(s) it delivers; Predecessors = the WI id(s) it depends on (the DAG edges; ;-joined) - a bare id is a hard edge (blocks; the acyclicity rule), a ~-prefixed id is a soft edge (advisory ordering; must resolve, never blocks, dashed in the render); Status = queued|active|done|deferred|blocked|retired (blocked requires a BlockRef reason; retired is a TERMINAL won't-build row that stays in the registry forever with its reason in Deliverable - counted separately from done, never scheduled, SpecRef cleared). SSOT model (process-options.md 'Trajectory / work-items layer'): Deliverable is backward-only - EMPTY while open, filled with what shipped at close (R-A); SpecRef is the forward bridge - a docs/specs/WI-###.md file or a doc#anchor that resolves while the WI is open and clears at close (R-E). BuildTier = an OPTIONAL per-WI routing hint for the unattended coordinator (process-options.md 'Unattended operation') - strong|medium|quick (legacy weak reads as quick); the STARTING build tier for THIS work item's BUILD sessions, read from the WI row by the dispatcher and composing with tier-up-never-down escalation (never caps escalation); empty/absent = the phase-default routing (never-breaking, read by name). CritiqueBudget and CritiqueExhaustion are OPTIONAL per-WI critique-loop controls: budget is a positive integer or inf; exhaustion is move-on or block; absent preserves the global default + move-on. The OPTIONAL parallel-dispatch scheduler columns are Priority (integer, default 0 - deliberate ordering within a gate class), Exclusive (;-joined semantic mutex keys; empty = optimistic), BlockRef (required when Status=blocked), EstTokens (draft-time size estimate), and SafetyClass (ordinary|spine|gate|attestation|protected|high-risk; absent fails closed as unclassified so a repo enables parallelism only after its SafetyClass audit), PlanMode (dual opts a WI into dual-plan decomposition; empty = normal build), and Bar (optional DevStg-Reqs|DevStg-Tests|DevStg-Impl: bar declares verification strictness for this row's lane; it never affects scheduling) - a legacy CSV without them reads every value as its default. status.md is an integrator-generated snapshot; the registry is authoritative. Execution 'how' atop the spine's 'what' - a view, never a source of truth. This -000 row is inert (check_trajectory.py ignores it), so a fresh scaffold stays vacuously clean.\",docs/specs/WI-000.md,,,,,,,,,,,\n+WI-000,EXAMPLE - delete on first real entry,workstream-name,SR-000,,queued,\"How the work executes (the artifact this ships). Workstream = the mutable grouping category the work advances (the legacy 'Track' header is still read); SR-Refs = the requirement id(s) it delivers; Predecessors = the id(s) it depends on (the DAG edges; ;-joined) - a bare WI-### id is a hard edge (blocks; the acyclicity rule), a ~-prefixed WI id is a soft edge (advisory ordering; must resolve, never blocks, dashed in the render), and a bare OI-### id is a hard edge on an open-item ruling (blocks until that open item leaves pending; an open item is not a DAG node, so it is not subject to the acyclicity rule) - the grammar widened tolerantly (O